00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:19.320
So let's talk about H2O-3
first, our open source platform.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.380
What is H2O?

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:23.290
So H2O is essentially
a math platform,

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:26.230
which provides basically an
open source and memory AI

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:29.250
engine that provides a
whole bunch of parallelized

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.050
and distributed algorithms--

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.990
popular ones like GLM, Random
Forest, GBM, Deep Learning,

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:35.130
et cetera.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.530
It's also an API
written in Java,

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.540
so that programmers can
take advantage of it

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.657
inside their applications.

00:04:41.657 --> 00:04:42.990
It's a very lightweight install.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.650
It can work on any environment,
where basic Java environment

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:47.880
can support it.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.230
It is built for big data.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.480
You can build lots
of models with it,

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:56.830
and you can use as much data
as you have without sampling.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.895
So the algorithms in H2O, if you
take a look at it for a second,

00:05:00.895 --> 00:05:03.070
we covered the most
commonly used machine

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.650
learning algorithms, including
statistical algorithms

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:08.680
like linear models,
naive Bayes--

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.690
an ensemble treatment--
there's, like,

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:13.979
Random Forest, Gradient
Boosting Machines,

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:16.270
and we also have a bunch of
Stacking and Super Learning

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:17.940
algorithms built in.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:20.290
We also support deep learning
deep neural networks,

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:25.600
including autoencoders, people
on neural networks, et cetera.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.420
On the right, you see a bunch
of unsupervised learning

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:31.120
algorithms, including
clustering methods like K-means.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.050
We also support dimensionality
reduction techniques like PCA

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.500
and generalized low rank models.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.205
We also have NLP supported
through Word2Vec and time

00:05:41.205 --> 00:05:43.440
series algorithm called ISax.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.250
In addition, all
of our algorithms

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:50.550
do have a wide variety of
hyper parameters to tune for.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.190
We offer early stopping
and AutoML functionality,

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:56.800
so that you can tune through
all the different algorithms

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.151
and get an ensemble
of the best models.

00:06:00.151 --> 00:06:01.650
So if you take a
look, for a minute,

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:04.620
at the typical enterprise
machine learning workflow,

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:07.291
you have data simply
sitting in different silos.

00:06:07.291 --> 00:06:08.790
They are all being
brought together,

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.484
and after you perform some
kind of detailed operation,

00:06:11.484 --> 00:06:13.400
the data quality and
transformation techniques

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:14.936
are then applied on it.

00:06:14.936 --> 00:06:16.310
And then you have,
what you call,

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.140
a modeling ready
data frame, where

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:20.820
you do feature engineering--

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.560
which can often
mean transformation

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.420
of existing features
into better features

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.420
that are more adaptable for
machine learning algorithms.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.130
And after that,
you then typically

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:32.100
spend time doing machine
learning and trying

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:35.720
out different algorithms, and
you have final model artifact.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:39.360
So how does H2O fit
into this workflow?

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.990
So with H2O, you can ingest
data from a wide variety

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:46.890
of data sources, including most
common on-prem and Cloud data

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:48.200
sources.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.760
So if you have data sitting in
HTFS or Google Cloud Storage,

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:52.500
for example, or
any other database,

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.460
you can pull data into different
connectors that we support.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.750
Once data is brought
into H2O, then it's

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:00.750
basically converted into
a distributed key value

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:03.390
store that's attached
to a data frame.

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:06.030
Once data is in there, you
can apply a whole bunch

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:08.910
of exploratory techniques,
feature engineering and model

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:11.215
building algorithms,
all of which

00:07:11.215 --> 00:07:12.840
are fully parallelized
and distributed.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.490
And that's the sum
of the platform.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:17.000
For the user, they can
use this through the REST

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:22.080
API, which in turn lets you
use R, or Python, or H2O Flow.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.940
Or if you're a programmer,
you can directly

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.620
work from Java or Scala.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.730
But for the end user, it's all
fully abstracted in [? DC ?]

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:31.590
and single H2O frame, where
they can operate on and apply

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:33.370
all these different techniques.

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.520
Once you run through all
the different models,

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.580
then you can essentially
export the model as a POJO

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.840
or as a MOJO, which are
basically Java artifacts that

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:43.110
encapsulate an entire model.

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:45.810
And these artifacts are
ready to go into production.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.694
You can deploy them into
a streaming environment.

00:07:48.694 --> 00:07:50.610
Put them in, like, a
serverless architecture--

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.280
Lambda architecture
if you want--

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:55.657
or you can take the code and
embed into any application

00:07:55.657 --> 00:07:56.490
that you might have.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:02.120
So how does that fit into
the Kubeflow paradigm?

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:05.110
So typically, in H2O,
you might spin up

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:07.120
a bunch of machines--
bare metal machines--

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:10.894
and create an H2O cluster,
where you run the H2O models.

00:08:10.894 --> 00:08:12.310
Instead of doing
that, if you were

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:16.104
to use Kubeflow as
an abstraction layer

00:08:16.104 --> 00:08:17.520
above the bare
metal and the H2O--

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.120
what that lets you
do is essentially

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:20.620
a team of data
scientists can all

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:25.840
share the same Kubeflow and
Kubernetes cluster to create

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:27.980
multiple H2O clusters.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:31.150
And these clusters can all
be running in parallel,

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:33.610
so multiple data scientists
can spin up a H2O cluster,

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:35.230
do their job, submit
their workloads,

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:38.320
and then turn it off,
and keep doing that.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.549
It also allows you to
scale up if you want to.

00:08:40.549 --> 00:08:41.809
For example, if your
workloads increase

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:44.225
you can have more nodes added
to the underlying Kubernetes

00:08:44.225 --> 00:08:46.540
or Kubeflow cluster.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:50.909
This can obviously run on
top of Cloud or on-prem.

00:08:50.909 --> 00:08:52.450
Now let's get to
Driverless AI, which

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:55.086
is our commercial
enterprise software.

00:08:55.086 --> 00:08:56.210
What does Driverless AI do?

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:59.320
Driverless AI delivers an
expert data scientist in a box.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:02.420
What we did is we created
an expert AI system, which

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:04.990
was built by the world's
most top Kaggle masters

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:08.500
and AI experts, and the goal
was to empower companies

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:10.600
to accomplish end-to-end
machine learning and data

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.370
science with a single platform.

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:15.100
It performs the work of a data
scientist in the sense that it

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.683
does all the feature engineering
and model-building activities

00:09:17.683 --> 00:09:19.810
that an expert data
scientist would perform

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:22.120
and gives you a really
highly accurate model which

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:23.930
is ready to go to production.

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:26.260
We also use a lot of different
visualization techniques

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:28.690
and interpretability
techniques to help understand

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:30.700
the results that are
created from the model,

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:35.134
so that you can build
trust in your model.

00:09:35.134 --> 00:09:37.300
So if you will take a look
at the typical enterprise

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.790
machine learning workflow
that we saw earlier,

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:42.550
what Driverless AI does
now is it automates

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:44.732
the entire workflow
from the point when

00:09:44.732 --> 00:09:45.940
you had the data frame ready.

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:49.000
So the feature engineering,
model building, deployment,

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:50.530
and interpretability,
they're all

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:52.210
taken care of by Driverless AI.

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:54.340
So from data ingested
to production,

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:56.194
we automated the
entire workflow.

00:09:56.194 --> 00:09:58.110
And this is, of course,
our marketing software

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:03.670
which won the InfoWorld
Technology of the Year Award.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:06.520
So why is Driverless AI a game
changer for enterprises, right?

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:11.020
So if you think about the three
common problems for enterprise

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:13.410
AI option, it's the
lack of AI talent.

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:16.150
There's a serious trade
of experienced expert data

00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:17.509
scientists.

00:10:17.509 --> 00:10:19.300
It takes a lot of time
for data scientists,

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:21.739
even the ones who are
trained and experienced,

00:10:21.739 --> 00:10:22.405
to build models.

00:10:22.405 --> 00:10:26.080
It can take anywhere from weeks
to days to build these models,

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:29.570
and the models they build are
often black box models which

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:30.820
are really tough to interpret.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:34.090
So businesses have a tough
time trusting these models

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:35.420
in production.

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:37.660
So what do we do with
Driverless AI is essentially

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:41.010
solve all these three problems
by offering a digital data

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:43.370
scientist-- essentially a
data scientist in a box.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:45.486
We automate the entire
machine learning workflow,

00:10:45.486 --> 00:10:47.110
using GPU accelerated
machine learning,

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:51.190
so that the time to build a
model goes from weeks to hours.

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:54.250
So we are drastically
reducing the time taken.

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.470
And finally, we do
offer explanations

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:58.060
and interpretations
for all the models

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:00.640
that we build in Driverless
AI, so that helps

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.220
build trust and transparency.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:07.120
So you can put these molds into
production knowing how and why

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:10.520
a prediction was being made.

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:14.790
So when we look at Driverless
AI, how well do we perform?

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:17.829
We took Driverless AI to task
by letting it participate

00:11:17.829 --> 00:11:19.370
in some of these
Kaggle competitions,

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:22.040
and what we found is
that out of the box,

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:24.650
the Driverless AI comes
in the top five percentile

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:25.580
in most competitions.

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:26.954
And in some
competitions, it does

00:11:26.954 --> 00:11:29.100
come in the top one percentile.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:31.520
And if you look at the people
who beat Driverless AI,

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:35.270
all the data scientist experts
took days and weeks of work,

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:37.930
whereas Driverless AI was
able to match the performance

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:39.119
in a matter of hours.

00:11:39.119 --> 00:11:41.160
So it provides this
automatic feature engineering

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:46.842
model building to give you the
most accuracy for your models.

00:11:46.842 --> 00:11:48.300
So what kind of
feature engineering

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:49.230
do we perform, right?

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:50.859
Very quickly, if
you look at it--

00:11:50.859 --> 00:11:53.400
I know Nick is going to cover
that in a demo in a little bit,

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:54.858
but essentially we
are doing things

00:11:54.858 --> 00:11:57.360
like automatic text
handling, frequency encoding,

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:00.930
cross-validated target encoding,
truncated SVD, clustering.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:02.520
These are things
that experienced

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.650
and expert data
scientists would do time

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:08.034
and again to help improve
the accuracy of their models.

00:12:08.034 --> 00:12:09.450
You apply the
feature engineering,

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.950
then do model building, and
then see which features came out

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:12.715
as significant.

00:12:12.715 --> 00:12:14.340
And you do this
iteratively, and that's

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:15.548
what Driverless AI automates.

00:12:15.548 --> 00:12:17.780
It goes through this
entire process iteratively

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:21.750
and gives you the power of an
experienced data scientist.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:24.030
And finally, let's talk a
minute about the deployment.

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:26.450
Whether it be H2O-3
or Driverless AI,

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:28.740
we always offer full
deployment options.

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.900
Every model built in either
in H2O or Driverless AI

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.420
comes with the JAVA artifact.

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:36.810
It's either a POJO
or MOJO file that's

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:38.880
ready to go into production.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:42.270
These artifacts are built for
a low latency inferencing,

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:45.630
so we are able to do some
millisecond inferencing.

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:47.490
And these models
can then be deployed

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:49.410
into different environments.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:52.590
They can be put on
the cloud or on-prem.

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:54.364
Put it behind a
REST API service,

00:12:54.364 --> 00:12:56.280
so your applications can
take advantage of it.

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:58.321
So we go from the entire
lifecycle model training

00:12:58.321 --> 00:13:00.980
management and deployment.

