and it will actually do--

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:03.240
it will give you--

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:04.500
the code is all self-baked.

00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:07.470
And all you really have to do is
just add some small parameters

00:26:07.470 --> 00:26:09.840
rather than writing
in R or Python,

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:12.760
so you don't necessarily
need to know those languages.

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:15.060
Now, if you were
interested in using Python,

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:18.420
then there is the Python Client.

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:21.300
And I've pre-spawned
a Jupyter notebook.

00:26:21.300 --> 00:26:23.370
Basically, all you have
to do is import H2O,

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:26.640
so this has to be
installed locally.

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:29.370
And from H2O, you can do--

00:26:29.370 --> 00:26:31.680
from H2O.out.

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:37.485
I'll import H2O AutoML.

00:26:37.485 --> 00:26:41.880
And this is just one of the
packages AutoML is basically

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:46.260
a package that will run
a very in-depth grid

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:49.350
search across multiple
different algorithms

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:54.660
in order to find the ones
that are most effective,

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:57.090
essentially, across your data.

00:26:57.090 --> 00:27:00.000
So from here, now that we've
actually imported H2O--

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:02.070
and you can actually take
a look through H2O Flow

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:05.370
here if I do admin and
get cluster status.

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:08.400
You can see that there's
actually three nodes attached,

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:11.500
and you can see some
available memory,

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:15.330
which is the amount of memory
that I've allocated previously.

00:27:15.330 --> 00:27:19.680
And we'll actually see this
once I connect here as well.

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:25.450
So h2o.init port is equal to--

00:27:25.450 --> 00:27:30.470
and I'm just going to have to
copy, paste the port again,

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:41.830
or rather the IP and the port,
which is this one right here.

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:52.780
Oh, there we go.

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:55.950
So basically, this is pre-baked.

00:27:55.950 --> 00:27:59.790
It's just called the cluster,
but this can be renamed.

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:01.620
As you can see, again, 3 nodes--

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:03.270
the total free
memory, which should

00:28:03.270 --> 00:28:05.580
aggregate to close to 18.

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:08.280
But there is a small
drop off because you

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:12.780
want to not use the maximum
heap memory for H2O-3

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:14.430
because it's written in Java.

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:20.680
So Java actually takes away
about 10% for itself to run.

00:28:20.680 --> 00:28:23.680
So at this point,
you're connected.

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:25.350
And you can do
basically the same thing

00:28:25.350 --> 00:28:31.030
as you could do in H2O Flow
but just with the Python

00:28:31.030 --> 00:28:31.530
interpreter.

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:37.080
So you can do train is
equal to H2O.ImportFile,

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:40.110
which would be able
to get you your data

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:43.620
if you pass it from GCS.

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:44.740
There is a GCS connector.

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:47.310
Now, this is an
open source product,

00:28:47.310 --> 00:28:49.080
so the GCS connector
is actually built

00:28:49.080 --> 00:28:51.540
by an external party,
from what I have heard,

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:55.710
and is getting merged soon.

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:58.110
But, you know, there's
also s3 connectors,

00:28:58.110 --> 00:29:02.880
as well as from local
source, and most

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:06.870
anywhere as long as it's
openly available for H2O

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:09.840
to connect and grab.

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:13.830
At the moment, unfortunately,
there was a minor bug

00:29:13.830 --> 00:29:17.190
that we were dealing
with previous to this

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:22.290
just this morning, so I haven't
been able to import any data.

00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:24.930
But we can take
a quick look back

00:29:24.930 --> 00:29:28.530
at our experiment, which
should have finished already.

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:29.680
Validation set is missing.

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:35.380
So it seems the
validation set that's here

00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:39.314
doesn't have the necessary
column, so we're actually

00:29:39.314 --> 00:29:40.480
just going to have to rerun.

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:48.079
Or we can rerun the
experiment, but we

00:29:48.079 --> 00:29:49.620
can go and describe
this really quick

00:29:49.620 --> 00:29:50.828
while we're going through it.

00:29:54.210 --> 00:29:56.730
Just a quick understanding--
a little bit more about what's

00:29:56.730 --> 00:30:00.360
going on here is that there's
a couple of tuning knobs--

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:02.640
basically, accuracy,
which is how highly do you

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:04.870
want to rate accuracy?

00:30:04.870 --> 00:30:06.060
So 10 would be the highest.

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:07.380
One would be the least.

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:09.690
And the model
itself will actually

00:30:09.690 --> 00:30:10.800
be built based on this.

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:13.620
So if you set a
low accuracy, it's

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:15.190
going to prioritize
running quicker.

00:30:15.190 --> 00:30:21.210
It's going to prioritize
making a more simple model.

00:30:21.210 --> 00:30:24.010
Time is also somewhat
dependent on the other,

00:30:24.010 --> 00:30:26.700
but basically meaning if
you allow it 10 for time,

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:29.490
it means you don't mind
it running forever--

00:30:29.490 --> 00:30:31.750
or not forever but for
a much longer time--

00:30:31.750 --> 00:30:35.610
and then you're probably likely
preferring more complex models

00:30:35.610 --> 00:30:37.510
and higher accuracy.

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:42.187
So that's what Driverless
AI will prioritize.

00:30:42.187 --> 00:30:43.770
And finally, there's
interpretability.

00:30:43.770 --> 00:30:46.270
Interpretability is essentially
the difference between, say,

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:53.910
having a stacked ensemble model
of 10 XGBoost models or maybe

00:30:53.910 --> 00:30:57.030
one-- or the idea being the
difference between having

00:30:57.030 --> 00:31:00.486
a neural net or a
linear regression--

00:31:00.486 --> 00:31:02.860
so linear regressions obviously
being more interpretable.

00:31:02.860 --> 00:31:04.943
So that would be if your
interpretability was 10--

00:31:04.943 --> 00:31:07.480
versus one might be
something more complicated--

00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:09.360
a neural net or a
stacked ensemble

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:11.940
of several XGBoost models.

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:14.740
So you can actually see
what's going on here.

00:31:14.740 --> 00:31:18.750
So right now we have a couple
different charts, but basically

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:22.360
this just shows, you know,
how much lift you're gaining--

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:24.180
your precision versus
a recall because this

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:25.860
is a classification--

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:27.480
of your rock curve.

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:32.070
And this is across
the number of epochs--

00:31:32.070 --> 00:31:36.262
just the performance that
the machine is outputting

00:31:36.262 --> 00:31:37.470
against your validation data.

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:41.220
So because our validation
data set was missing a column,

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:45.300
it's actually going to just
do cross-validation splits.

00:31:45.300 --> 00:31:50.310
So you're basically taking a
section out of the training

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:55.230
data set and running
on each iteration that

00:31:55.230 --> 00:32:00.636
has the validation score.

00:32:00.636 --> 00:32:02.010
So that's what
we're seeing here.

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:03.660
It's not just
taking the data set

00:32:03.660 --> 00:32:06.960
and training itself
on it over and over.

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:09.450
So what's really cool here
is the variable importance,

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:12.090
and this is something that
Vinod highlighted earlier.

00:32:12.090 --> 00:32:14.940
Essentially, what it is is
that you can see a couple

00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:16.410
columns, which are like Pay 1.

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:19.950
This is actually a column that
exists in the normal data set,

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:24.540
but there's also other
data sets like CVTE Pay 1,

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:29.010
which is the cross-validated
target encoding for that

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:31.090
specific column--

00:32:31.090 --> 00:32:33.630
which I believe that's
actually Pay 10.

00:32:33.630 --> 00:32:34.440
Excuse me.

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:36.360
So that's the cross-validated
target encoding

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:38.700
of the column Pay 10.

00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:41.070
So the difference is is
that one was actually

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:44.820
generated by the model and
by Driverless AI itself--

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:47.190
versus Pay 1, which was
something that just came along

00:32:47.190 --> 00:32:51.510
with the data set.

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:53.490
And since this is a
very small data set,

00:32:53.490 --> 00:32:55.470
it seems trained fairly quickly.

00:32:55.470 --> 00:32:57.330
And once it completes,
it will actually

00:32:57.330 --> 00:33:03.660
train a final model that can
be output as a MOJO or POJO.

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:07.950
So we can actually wait for
that to go since it's going

00:33:07.950 --> 00:33:09.760
to finish in just a second.

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:11.010
And the last thing is--

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:13.350
after this is trained--
you can actually run MLI.

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:16.050
MLI stands for Machine
Learning Interpretability.

00:33:16.050 --> 00:33:20.640
And what it does is it basically
takes you or more complex model

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:23.790
if you were to, say,
have an accuracy of 10

00:33:23.790 --> 00:33:25.290
and an interpretability
of 1, you

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:27.000
can actually take
that model afterwards,

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:30.060
and it will run several--

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:31.770
the equivalent of,
like, several linear

00:33:31.770 --> 00:33:37.980
regressions to match what the
more complex XGBoost model is

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:39.480
doing under the hood.

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:41.400
So that you can actually
see what's going on--

00:33:41.400 --> 00:33:42.960
see the reasoning
and the decision

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:46.210
making behind our
more complex model.

00:33:46.210 --> 00:33:48.510
For, in many cases,
like certain verticals

00:33:48.510 --> 00:33:52.545
like the financial vertical,
this would be very important.

00:33:52.545 --> 00:33:54.920
So from here you can actually
see-- so this would be MLI.

00:33:54.920 --> 00:33:56.650
If you clicked
Interpret this Model,

00:33:56.650 --> 00:33:58.766
it'll actually start
interpreting it.

00:33:58.766 --> 00:34:00.640
I can actually close
that while it's running,

00:34:00.640 --> 00:34:03.510
so MLI is running right now.

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:07.410
And we can actually just
go back to the experiment

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:08.080
and take a look.

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:10.710
So this is where you might
want to download an experiment

00:34:10.710 --> 00:34:12.810
summary, download some logs--

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:14.550
if you build the
MOJO scoring pipeline

00:34:14.550 --> 00:34:17.940
in this or the Python scoring
pipeline, which would be POJO,

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:22.120
this is where you would
get the deployment that you

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:24.150
would be able to deploy.

00:34:24.150 --> 00:34:26.520
Quite probably the best
place would be in the Cloud

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:30.210
or on Kubernetes where
it's scalable to demand,

00:34:30.210 --> 00:34:33.190
and you could use it
to make predictions.

00:34:33.190 --> 00:34:37.620
So it looks like we're running
out of time, so I think--

00:34:37.620 --> 00:34:42.090
stay tuned for a live Q&amp;A. We'll
be back in less than a minute.

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:47.070
And welcome back with our
Q&amp;A. So the first question

00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:49.950
is, what machine learning
platforms can run on Kubeflow,

00:35:49.950 --> 00:35:51.880
and is it limited to TensorFlow?

00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:53.220
The answer is no.

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:54.840
It's not limited to TensorFlow.

00:35:54.840 --> 00:35:57.600
In fact, I believe
in the Kubeflow repo

00:35:57.600 --> 00:36:01.260
there's several different
packages already

00:36:01.260 --> 00:36:06.060
there as well, inclusive
Caffe, Karas, I believe,

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:10.650
as well as TensorFlow for deep
learning with machine learning.

00:36:10.650 --> 00:36:16.740
This is what H2O does, and we're
running on Kubeflow right now.

00:36:16.740 --> 00:36:21.090
So that's probably, I would say,
there's a lot of opportunities

00:36:21.090 --> 00:36:21.800
right here.

00:36:21.800 --> 00:36:24.360
H2O has been leveraging
it for the better

00:36:24.360 --> 00:36:27.600
of a couple of months since
we started the partnership,

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:30.780
and it seems to run fairly well.

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:33.510
So yeah, H2O-3 as another
option for machine

00:36:33.510 --> 00:36:35.490
learning on Kubeflow.

00:36:35.490 --> 00:36:36.990
ANTHONY HYLICK: So
the next question

00:36:36.990 --> 00:36:38.910
is can I run Kubeflow on-prem?

00:36:38.910 --> 00:36:41.010
Absolutely, that was
part of the design

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:42.680
around something
that was portable

00:36:42.680 --> 00:36:44.589
and for distributed
machine learning.

00:36:44.589 --> 00:36:45.630
You could run it on-prem.

00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:47.546
You could run it on your
laptop via mini cube.

00:36:47.546 --> 00:36:48.840
You can run it in the Cloud.

00:36:48.840 --> 00:36:50.840
Anywhere you have
Kubernetes, the goal

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:54.230
is to be able to run Kubeflow.

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:56.330
The next question
we have is-- what

00:36:56.330 --> 00:36:58.510
is the security privacy
compliance for Kubeflow

00:36:58.510 --> 00:36:59.390
running on the cloud?

00:36:59.390 --> 00:37:02.690
Is this any different
than running a VM?

00:37:02.690 --> 00:37:05.750
There are no additional
security or privacy concerns

00:37:05.750 --> 00:37:07.550
that Kubeflow opens up.

00:37:07.550 --> 00:37:11.630
So any of the same container
or VM concerns that you have,

00:37:11.630 --> 00:37:14.780
these are going to
maintain-- those concerns are

00:37:14.780 --> 00:37:19.310
going to stay the same
when you use Kubeflow.

00:37:19.310 --> 00:37:20.790
And the last
question we have is--

00:37:20.790 --> 00:37:24.180
can I use you GPU/TPU with
Kubeflow running on GCP?

00:37:24.180 --> 00:37:26.580
Absolutely, you
can use both GPUs

00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:29.314
or you can use TPUs, whichever
you need for the work

00:37:29.314 --> 00:37:31.230
that you need to get
done, or the performance,

00:37:31.230 --> 00:37:32.810
or the accuracy
that you're after.

00:37:32.810 --> 00:37:35.660
GPUs and TPUs are
available for those cases.

00:37:38.470 --> 00:37:40.720
NICHOLAS PNG: Actually, if
I could elaborate once more

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:42.250
on question number two.

00:37:42.250 --> 00:37:45.010
So running on-prem
for with Kubeflow--

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:48.375
one of the things that we
are currently working on--

00:37:48.375 --> 00:37:50.480
a prototype, if you will--

00:37:50.480 --> 00:37:53.170
with the Kubeflow
team is actually

00:37:53.170 --> 00:37:55.415
the opportunity to
do burst to cloud,

00:37:55.415 --> 00:37:57.040
which would mean that
you start on-prem

00:37:57.040 --> 00:37:59.980
with, say, four machines, or
three machines, or however

00:37:59.980 --> 00:38:00.770
many.

00:38:00.770 --> 00:38:07.159
And if your demand does
exceed the amount of resources

00:38:07.159 --> 00:38:09.700
available on-prem, then you can
actually just spin up another

00:38:09.700 --> 00:38:12.980
machine that will be on GCP--

00:38:12.980 --> 00:38:16.360
so through GCE.

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:19.420
And it will attach itself
to the Kubernetes cluster

00:38:19.420 --> 00:38:24.340
and then act as a new resource
for the Kubernetes cluster.

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:27.430
And then basically it'd
be a first-class citizen

00:38:27.430 --> 00:38:31.840
for being spun down,
ultimately, once the resources

00:38:31.840 --> 00:38:33.754
load is reduced.

00:38:33.754 --> 00:38:35.670
So that's actually
something we're working on.

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:38.110
We actually have
a couple scripts

00:38:38.110 --> 00:38:41.650
that are on the H2O
Kubeflow repo that

00:38:41.650 --> 00:38:45.210
are kind of the
prototypes of this,

00:38:45.210 --> 00:38:49.730
and they will continue to be
updated as time progresses.

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:51.730
ANTHONY HYLICK: Yeah,
absolutely, burst to cloud

00:38:51.730 --> 00:38:53.920
enables dynamic
capacity increase

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:55.971
and decrease as your
workload needs it.

00:38:55.971 --> 00:38:58.230
NICHOLAS PNG: Yeah.

00:38:58.230 --> 00:39:00.100
ANTHONY HYLICK: So
that's it for Q&amp;A.

00:39:00.100 --> 00:39:01.940
I guess, stay tuned
for the next session,

